# Summary
The new AuthorizationProfile was not consistently enforced. Functions like requester_can_read_object and requester_can_manage_object_tags still authorize any same-account requester when ownership is BucketOwnerEnforced, so a Standard-profile credential can read objects and modify tags despite being intended as constrained. This is an authorization bypass introduced with the new profile model.
The commit introduces AuthorizationProfile to distinguish owner-account administrators from constrained same-account credentials. However, several authorization paths still use requester_is_bucket_owner_account without checking the profile. In particular, when bucket ownership controls are BucketOwnerEnforced (the default for new buckets), requester_can_read_object returns true for any same-account principal, so a constrained credential can read objects without ACL or policy grants. The same issue affects object tag management, which also treats any same-account principal as authorized. This bypasses the intended restrictions for Standard credentials and allows unauthorized data access within the same account.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Identify AuthorizationProfile semantics (Standard vs OwnerAccountAdmin) in auth credential definition
- [x] Confirm requester_can_bucket_owner_account_admin uses authorization_profile gating
- [x] Verify requester_can_read_object BucketOwnerEnforced branch uses requester_is_bucket_owner_account without profile check
- [x] Verify requester_can_manage_object_tags uses requester_is_bucket_owner_account without profile check
- [x] Confirm requester_is_bucket_owner_account only checks account/principal equality
## Report
Build attempt for dynamic validation failed because the ec-sys crate requires the system libisal dependency (see /workspace/validation_artifacts/argmin-authz-bypass/build_attempt.log: cargo build stops with “libisal not found”). Valgrind and gdb are not installed in the container, so crash/valgrind/debugger validation could not be performed.

Code inspection confirms the bypass. AuthorizationProfile::Standard is documented as “No implicit owner-account administrative permissions” while OwnerAccountAdmin has broad owner-account permissions (crates/auth/src/credential.rs:6-13). The intended profile gating is implemented in requester_can_bucket_owner_account_admin, which only grants same-account admin rights when authorization_profile == OwnerAccountAdmin (crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:64-70). However, requester_can_read_object short-circuits for BucketOwnerEnforced buckets to requester_is_bucket_owner_account without any profile check (crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:174-181). requester_is_bucket_owner_account only compares principals/account IDs (crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:295-314), so any same-account credential (including Standard) is treated as authorized. The same pattern exists in requester_can_manage_object_tags, which allows any same-account requester via requester_is_bucket_owner_account (crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:404-411). This matches the reported authorization bypass: Standard-profile credentials gain read/tag management capabilities in owner-enforced buckets despite the profile’s intended constraints.

