# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: low | Policy adjusted: low
## Rationale
Impact is unauthorized data access and tag modification within the same account (not cross-tenant) and requires a valid same-account Standard credential. BucketOwnerEnforced is the default for CreateBucket, making the path likely when constrained credentials are used. Overall probability and impact align with medium severity rather than high/critical.
## Likelihood
medium - Exploitation is straightforward once a Standard-profile same-account credential exists; however, such constrained credentials are optional and not created by default in main configuration.
## Impact
medium - A same-account Standard-profile credential can read private object data and manage object tags without ACL/policy grants under BucketOwnerEnforced buckets, violating intended privilege boundaries within the account.
## Assumptions
- A same-account credential is provisioned with AuthorizationProfile::Standard (constrained credentials are used in deployment).
- Affected buckets use BucketOwnerEnforced ownership controls (the default when x-amz-object-ownership is omitted).
- The S3 HTTP endpoint is reachable by the attacker (ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR may be set beyond localhost).
- Valid SigV4 credential for the same AWS account with AuthorizationProfile::Standard
- Bucket ownership controls set to BucketOwnerEnforced (default for CreateBucket without x-amz-object-ownership)
## Path
attacker(Standard cred) -> S3 HTTP -> BucketOwnerEnforced short-circuit -> object data/tags
## Path evidence
- `crates/auth/src/credential.rs:6-13` - AuthorizationProfile::Standard is defined as having no implicit owner-account administrative permissions, while OwnerAccountAdmin has broad permissions.
- `crates/auth/src/request.rs:385-390` - AuthContext carries authorization_profile from the credential record used for SigV4 authentication.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:784-788` - HTTP layer passes AuthContext.authorization_profile into core Requester via from_account_with_profile.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:174-181` - BucketOwnerEnforced path in requester_can_read_object returns requester_is_bucket_owner_account without AuthorizationProfile gating.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:404-411` - requester_can_manage_object_tags allows any same-account principal via requester_is_bucket_owner_account.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs:295-309` - requester_is_bucket_owner_account only compares principals/account IDs, not authorization profile.
- `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:5062-5067` - parse_bucket_ownership defaults to BucketOwnerEnforced when header is absent.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/config.rs:24-62` - Default listen address is 127.0.0.1:9000; exposure depends on ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR.
## Narrative
AuthorizationProfile::Standard is documented as having no implicit owner-account permissions, yet BucketOwnerEnforced read/tagging paths grant access to any same-account principal. In requester_can_read_object, BucketOwnerEnforced short-circuits to requester_is_bucket_owner_account, which only checks account/principal match and ignores AuthorizationProfile. requester_can_manage_object_tags similarly allows any same-account principal. Because CreateBucket defaults to BucketOwnerEnforced when x-amz-object-ownership is absent, a Standard-profile same-account credential can read object data or manage tags without ACL or policy grants.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication
- ACL and public access block checks
- Bucket ownership controls
- Optional TLS
## Blindspots
- Static-only analysis; no runtime PoC due to build limitations.
- No deployment manifests to confirm whether ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR exposes the service publicly.
- Credential provisioning mechanisms beyond the single OwnerAccountAdmin in main are not shown; constrained credentials are inferred from tests and auth profile support.

# Remediation
Fixed in commit `c7dc864` (`Align BOE and GetObjectAttributes auth with AWS`).

The fix tightened BucketOwnerEnforced authorization in
`crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz.rs` so BOE-only implicit
owner-account access consistently requires
`requester_can_bucket_owner_account_admin(...)` rather than raw same-account
checks. It also narrowed owner-identity matching so an exact owner principal
still works for `Standard`, but a same-canonical-id different principal only
counts as owner on the intended `OwnerAccountAdmin` surfaces.

Follow-up coverage was added in:
- `crates/s3-tests/tests/boe_constrained.rs`
- `crates/s3-tests/tests/boe_admin_root.rs`
- `crates/s3-tests/tests/object_attributes_auth.rs`

Those suites now pin the AWS-observed BOE boundaries for constrained
same-account denial, intended owner-account-admin/root allow paths, and the
separate `GetObjectAttributes` permission/missing-key behavior.
